<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dordogne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dordogne is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in the region of Nouvelle-Aquitaine, roughly corresponding to the old province of Périgord, with its prefecture at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perigueux">Périgueux</a>. It takes its name from the Dordogne river, which crosses it from east to west. In antiquity it was the territory of the Gaulish Petrocorii, and its valleys hold some of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s best-known prehistoric sites, including the cave of Lascaux.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dordogne.jpg" alt="Dordogne" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Père Igor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C3%A9rigueux_pr%C3%A9fecture_(2).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
